[10:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'version where I used this reference video of me walking around my garden, and for this one I actually took the first frame of that video and created this image of myself in a hazmat suit. Then when uploading that image and the video of myself, this is the video from'

[11:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'C Dance 2. Now I think it looks much better than the version before. It does actually feel like you\'re watching a clip from the film, and yeah, I was really happy with how this one turned out. And here is a Gemini Omni version.'

[11:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'What I find sometimes with Gemini Omni is that they\'ll change the reference footage, and in this video here, it changed my face and it added some dialogue that I didn\'t say. So, I wasn\'t too impressed with that result. And here'

[11:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'was another version, but in a different suit using SeaDance 2. And again, I think it looks very clean, and yeah, just looks way better than the Gemini Omni version. I know they\'re just short'

[11:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'clips, but I feel like this kind of shot you could just stitch into the film, and you probably wouldn\'t even notice. Now, this shot here is very simple, but I wanted to try and recreate a shot from the Odyssey trailer. The shot is of Tom'

[11:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Holland on a boat. So, I\'ve got this reference footage of myself just copying that pose, then generated this image, which kind of looked similar to the trailer shot. And the final output from SeaDance 2, I think it looks incredible.'

[12:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'We\'ve got that kind of rocking from being on the boat. We\'ve got some subtle candle light as well. While it\'s very simple, I think these kind of shots work really well for these kind of video models. I have noticed a bit of a mistake as I\'ve left my Apple Watch on'

[12:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'my character. That\'s a bit of a Starbucks cup in Game of Thrones moment there. And here\'s a shot from the trailer, which I tried to replicate where the building is collapsing [music] behind him with using a bit of motion from my own reference video.'

[12:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now, here are a few more quick VFX shots that I\'ve done. One of the main VFX shots you find in films is destruction. So, I just took this image from outside the new stadium in Christchurch, uploaded it into Gemini Omni, and'

[12:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'prompted it with the frame starts to collapse. So, a very simple prompt. I think it looks really good. The destruction looks quite realistic and has a lot of dynamic action to it, and even the camera falls at the end as'

[13:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'well, which is a nice touch. And here are a couple from SeaDance 2. I think these ones do look better. I could definitely see TV shows or films in the future using this approach if you need to create a short shot of something'

[13:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'being destroyed. As if you\'re just chucking in a 3 to 5 second clip in a TV show, if I saw that, I\'m not going to look at it and say, "That\'s AI." I think it does look pretty realistic. So, I think these models work really well for'

[13:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'those kind of destruction shots. Now, this next effect I thought would not work at all, but it turned out to be one of the best ones I\'ve done yet. Do you remember that scene from the Pirates of the Caribbean film where the pirates on the Black Pearl were cursed and when'

[13:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'they came into contact, I think it was with the moonlight, they would look undead. But then outside of the moonlight, they would look real. So, I\'ve got this footage of myself where I dimmed down the lights and then just used this flashlight to kind of go over'

[13:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'my face. Then uploading that and using this prompt, this is the video I got from Gemini Omni. Now, it looked okay. I wasn\'t that happy with how the light goes over the face and it just kind of slowly fades into this undead look. And'

[14:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this is the result from SeaArt\'s 2. I think the effect looks way better when I move it across my face. But as the light exits, it does a kind of more popping effect back to my other face,'

[14:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'which I don\'t think looks that great. But if I go frame by frame, you can see as soon as the light hits my face, it is just changing those bits of the face with the light in it, which I was incredibly impressed with. Then I tried'

[14:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it again and it fixed that problem. As the light goes over the face, it changes it perfectly. And as the light exits the face, it goes back in the way that I wanted it to. So, if I move frame by frame as the light exits,'

[14:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it\'s blending back to the original face perfectly where the light isn\'t. I was really happy with how that one turned out. I thought this effect wouldn\'t work at all, to be honest, but as you can see'

[14:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'by those results, personally, I think it looks awesome. I think this shows you how creative you can get with the kind of shots that you can make with these models. Now, let\'s have a quick look at the director mode in OpenArt. Now, we come over to director here, you can'

[15:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'actually choose from one of these quick starts. So, as you can see, they will help guide you to create short films, music videos, product ads, film trailers. These are a great way to kind of get yourself introduced to director mode and how it all works. It is pretty'

[15:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'simple. You just type in what you want in this prompt box here. What director mode is is that you are chatting to a really smart AI bot that you can have a back and forth chat with to get the shot that you\'re looking for. So, you can'

[15:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'type in the video that you want in the prompt box here, and you can choose to have guide me or just make it. So, with the guide me approach, at each step it will kind of say to you, "Hey, do you want to run with this idea or this one?" And then it will give you some options'

[15:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'or you can give it a custom prompt. So, each process for the video, you\'re kind of chatting back and forth with the director to kind of guide that video. Whereas in the just a make it option, it will just go through all of the steps for you automatically and then deliver'

[15:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you a final video, which can be great if you just want to set and forget it. But personally, I do like the guide me approach as I like to have a bit of a conversation with the AI bot to really nail down the exact things that I want for the video. And here you can upload'

[16:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reference images. Now, there isn\'t an option to upload reference videos into the director mode, but I did find a workaround. So, the way I got around this was to actually upload my reference videos into Dropbox, and then in the'

[16:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'prompt box I would say, "I want to create this effect on my reference video. Here is the link to my reference video." And then I would just paste in that Dropbox link. And it actually goes and looks at that link, gets the video, and then it actually uses that video in'

[16:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the director mode. Hopefully, they do add a video upload in director mode soon, but that\'s just my hack on how to get around that. So, this is one of the tests I\'ve made in the director mode. I saw a cool trailer for the show called'

[16:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Human Vapor where there\'s this effect of smoke and vapor going into this man\'s mouth and he starts expanding. I wanted to try recreate that kind of effect. So, I\'ve got this stupid video of myself pretending that the vapor\'s going in my'

[17:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mouth, and I gave it this prompt of I want vapor to go in my mouth and then my body to expand. Now, you can actually prompt it to use different video models. So, as you can see here, I said use Seedance 2. Now, it did generate a video, and it looks pretty good. But as'

[17:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you can see, the smoke is coming out of my mouth, but I wanted it to go in. And I find this is where the director mode works really well. And you can have a bit of back and forth with it. And after that video got made, I said, "That looks'

[17:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'good, but the smoke vapor was coming out of his mouth. I want a continuous stream of smoke vapor going into his mouth and not out." I think the AI bot is called Ori. So, Ori got back to me and said, "Good catch. That\'s the exact flip I'

[17:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wanted about. The source is actually an exhale." So, the engine kept the smoke blowing outwards. Then it actually thinks of how to fix that effect that you\'re looking for, which I think is pretty smart. So, it came back with the smoke going in my mouth, which is quite'

[17:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a comical effect. But, I think that\'s a really good showcase on how to use the director mode, as I think I would struggle just prompting that without using director mode. But, it actually found a work-around on how to get the smoke going in rather than out. So, if'

[18:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you are struggling to get the effect that you\'re looking for, then I would definitely give a director mode a go and have a chat to it. Say exactly what you want, and it should find a way on how to make it work. So, looking at the pricing'

[18:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for OpenArt, these are the monthly plans. I would say the infinite and wonder plans give you the most flexibility on how many credits you get and how many videos you can make. It all depends on what kind of shots you\'re looking to create. If you are looking at'

[18:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'creating 4K C dense two videos, then the wonder plan will be the one that you want to go for. But, if you want to create videos for say social media, then the infinite plan might be better for that, as you can do shots with Gemini'

[18:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Omni, and I think a 10-second video from them is around 500 credits. I have probably used over $200 worth of credits making all of these examples that I\'ve showed you. Plus, I\'ve made way more'

[18:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that I didn\'t include. So, it does get quite costly. I think if you\'re working on a project and you want some decent effect shots, I will definitely think you will get your money\'s worth using one of these plans. If you are looking for the best value though, then the'

[19:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'annual plans will be your best choice. So, what are your thoughts on these video models for creating Hollywood-style visual effects? Now, I\'m not saying these are up to the standards of professional VFX artists, as I don\'t'

[19:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'think they\'re near that yet. I think looking how things have progressed over the last year, it\'s definitely got a lot better. I think we\'ll start to see over the next year or two where maybe big studios do start implementing these kind'

[19:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of video models for their effects work. I\'d like to know your thoughts in the comments section down below. If you\'d like to learn more and have a chat to me, then feel free to join my free school community. It would be awesome if you could give this video a like and'

[19:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'subscribe if you haven\'t yet. My name is Jack, and I will see you in the next one.'
